<!-- TYPES OF STORM DAMAGE -->
<section class="py-20" style="background:#F5F5F5;border-top:1px solid #E5E5E5;border-bottom:1px solid #E5E5E5">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-5">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="sl gp mb-3">Damage Types</p>
      <h2 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:800;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);line-height:.95;color:#1A1A1A;letter-spacing:-.01em">COMMON TYPES OF STORM DAMAGE IN FLORIDA</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><path d="M18 10h-1.26A8 8 0 1 0 9 20h9a5 5 0 0 0 0-10z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Wind Damage</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">Wind doesn't have to reach hurricane speed to do some real damage. Sustained tropical-storm-force winds can break the seal between shingle layers without visibly tearing anything off. Once that seal fails, water can work its way underneath during the next heavy rain, or tiles may shift just enough to expose the underlayment below.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><path d="M12 3v3m0 12v3m-7.8-4.2 2.1-2.1m9.6-9.6 2.1-2.1M3 12h3m12 0h3M4.2 4.2l2.1 2.1m9.6 9.6 2.1 2.1"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Hail Damage</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">Hail can knock the protective granules off asphalt shingles and leave exposed spots that could eventually allow water infiltration. On metal, it leaves dents that could compromise the panel's structural integrity, while tile may crack or completely shatter. What's tricky about hail damage is that it tends to look minor from the ground and likely won't show up as a leak until months later.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5z"/><path d="M2 17l10 5 10-5"/><path d="M2 12l10 5 10-5"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Debris Impact</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">Falling branches, flying debris, and anything the wind picks up can puncture roofing materials, crack tiles, dent metal panels, and damage gutters or fascia in ways that aren't always obvious until you're actually up there looking at it. Even a relatively small impact can create a water entry point that will do damage over time if it goes unaddressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2.69l5.66 5.66a8 8 0 1 1-11.31 0z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Water Intrusion</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">Heavy rain driven by wind doesn't need much of an opening to get through. It will work under lifted shingles and into any spot where sealant has cracked or pulled away. Once it's past the exterior, it saturates decking and insulation quickly, and in Florida's humidity, mold can start to grow within a day or two.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><path d="M3 9l9-7 9 7v11a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2z"/><polyline points="9 22 9 12 15 12 15 22"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Structural Damage</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">When a tree comes down on a roof or debris hits hard enough, the damage can go well beyond the surface. Cracked rafters and compromised decking don't always show from the outside, which is exactly why a professional inspection after a major storm matters even when things look okay from the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scard p-6">
        <div class="step-num mb-4">
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#E6A817" stroke-width="2"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M3 9h18"/><path d="M9 3v18"/></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">Flashing &amp; Sealant Failure</h3>
        <p style="color:#555555;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7">The flashing around chimneys, vents, skylights, and wall-to-roof transitions tends to take the brunt of heavy wind. If any of these start to pull away or crack open, they can become one of the most persistent leak sources on the entire roof, and they're often missed during surface-level assessments.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- INSURANCE CLAIMS PROCESS -->
<section class="py-20" style="background:#FFFFFF">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-5">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="sl gt mb-3">Insurance Claims</p>
      <h2 style="font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:800;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);line-height:.95;color:#1A1A1A;letter-spacing:-.01em">HOW WE CAN HELP WITH YOUR INSURANCE CLAIM</h2>
      <p style="color:#555555;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:1.25rem auto 0">
